# Line endings are a cross-platform question here, not a style one.
#
# A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true (the default from Git for Windows)
# rewrites every text file to CRLF. That is harmless for the C++ - the compiler
# does not care - but tests/smoke.sh is a shell script, and a shell script with
# CRLF fails on Linux and macOS at the first line: `set: -: invalid option`,
# then a syntax error at the end of the file. It only bites when a tree checked
# out on Windows is carried to a Unix host - a container, WSL, a copied
# directory - which is exactly what happens when someone tests the other
# platform from this one.
#
# So: normalise in the repository, and pin LF on checkout for the files where
# the interpreter is the one reading them.
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
